findings.body:
The new early return fires whenever `bucket_folder` appears **anywhere** in `path`, not only at the start:

::diff{file="src/integrations/prefect-gcp/prefect_gcp/cloud_storage.py" lines="734-735"}

Concrete failures:
- `bucket_folder="data/"`, `path="mydata/file"`: `"data/" in "mydata/file"` is True, so the path comes back unchanged and is written to the **bucket root** instead of `data/mydata/file`.
- `bucket_folder="results/"`, `path="archive/results/x"`: written to `archive/results/x`, outside the configured folder.

`read_path`, `write_path`, `get_directory` and `put_directory` all go through this function. Data can be silently written to, or read from, outside the block's folder. That's a data-isolation regression.

::callout{type="warning"}
The PR description says this is "the same fix" as `_join_bucket_folder` (#18237), but that helper correctly uses `bucket_path.startswith(self.bucket_folder)` (line 928).

::suggestion{file="src/integrations/prefect-gcp/prefect_gcp/cloud_storage.py" lines="734-735"}
        if self.bucket_folder and path.startswith(self.bucket_folder):
            return path

The old triple-quoted string wrote patterns with leading whitespace (`    testdir1/*`), so the ignore patterns never matched. The single-line `"testdir1/*\n.prefectignore\n"` fixes that, so the fixture now actually tests ignore behaviour.

::code{file="src/integrations/prefect-gcp/tests/projects/test_steps.py" lines="57-57"}

The `conftest.py` simplification also matches the prefect-aws and prefect-azure conftests.
